{{delete|reason=This image, which won at the 2023 Wiki Loves Monuments competition, was discussed at [[Commons:Village_pump/Copyright/Archive/2025/03#File:Giza Pyramids_during "Forever is Now" exhibition.jpg]]. It was thought that Egyptian FoP applied to the artwork authored by [[w:en:Lorenzo Quinn]]. However, as per [[Commons:Village pump/Copyright/Archive/2025/08#Egyptian FoP]] (a discussion which I opened), it appeared that the Egyptian FoP was not an independent legal right provision, but a provision ''dependent'' on personal use under Article 171(2) of the copyright law (not public distribution on the Internet).

The third parties, under Article 171(2) of the Egyptian copyright law, can "reproduce or copy works of fine, applied or plastic arts displayed in a public place, or works of architecture" but only by "making a single copy of the work for one's exclusive personal use," something that the author or his successor cannot control. Public distribution on the Internet is no longer an instance of personal use. Although one may argue that Quinn exhibits his artworks in different public exhibitions around the world from time to time, the non-permanence of his artworks means that even in countries with FoP rules like Germany, Belgium, the Netherlands, and Austria, photos of his works do not enjoy FoP privileges.

Therefore, a permission from '''Lorenzo Quinn''' for the usage of commercial Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 4.0 license on this image (which was the 2023 winner of Wiki Loves Monuments) is required for this winning image to be kept in public display (that the Egyptian's restrictive law does not permit in normal circumstances). "FoP-Egypt" is no longer a valid FoP tag as per [[Commons:Deletion requests/Template:FoP-Egypt]].|subpage=File:Giza Pyramids during "Forever is Now" exhibition.jpg|year=2026|month=August|day=28}}
